{"version_id": 46, "article_id": 5, "level_code": "ja_n5", "lang": "ja", "level": 1, "level_label": "N5 初級", "title": "石油会社が最高裁に「裁判を止めて」とたのむ", "title_zh": "石油公司请求最高法院叫停气候诉讼", "topic": "tech", "source": "arstechnica", "source_url": "https://arstechnica.com/tech-policy/2026/10/big-oil-asks-supreme-court-to-kill-climate-lawsuits-before-trial/", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://live.staticflickr.com/3913/14334624106_a9bcc306a9_b.jpg", "credit": "Bernard Spragg · cc0 1.0 · via flickr"}, "published_date": "2026-10-06", "word_count": 338, "reading_minutes": 1.1, "lead": "石油の会社が、気候についてうそをついたと言われています。会社は「裁判をしないでほしい」と最高裁にたのみました。", "lead_zh": "石油公司被指在气候问题上欺骗公众。公司方面请求最高法院不要让他们上法庭。", "preview": "アメリカの最高裁で、月曜日に話し合いがありました。エクソンとサンコーという石油の会社が、「州の法律で会社を裁くのはおかしい」と言いました。会社の弁護士は「一つの市が世界の石油のルールを決めることはできない」と言いました。", "preview_zh": "美国最高法院周一举行了辩论。埃克森和美孚等石油公司表示：“用州法律来审判公司是不合理的。”公司律师说：“一个城市不能决定全世界的石油规则。”", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 36, "url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/audio/5/ja_n5.mp3", "duration": 72.638, "size_bytes": 439488, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 251, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@a79b424/content/data/versions/46.json", "paragraphs": ["アメリカの最高裁で、月曜日に話し合いがありました。エクソンとサンコーという石油の会社が、「州の法律で会社を裁くのはおかしい」と言いました。会社の弁護士は「一つの市が世界の石油のルールを決めることはできない」と言いました。", "ボルダーという市は2018年に裁判を始めました。市は「会社は気候についてうそをついた」と言っています。市はお金を求めています。活動を止めてとは言っていません。市の弁護士は「これはうその話です」と言いました。", "裁判官の中には「今はまだ早い」と言う人もいました。アリート裁判官はその日いませんでした。かれは石油の株を持っているからです。判決は6月ごろになります。", "最高裁の外では、人が集まって「裁判を続けて」と言いました。ボルダーの市の人は「気候のせいで、火事や水や暑さがひどくなっています。お金は市民だけが払うのですか」と言いました。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["美国最高法院周一举行了辩论。埃克森和美孚等石油公司表示：“用州法律来审判公司是不合理的。”公司律师说：“一个城市不能决定全世界的石油规则。”", "博尔德市在2018年提起了诉讼。该市称：“公司在气候问题上撒了谎。”该市要求赔偿金，并没有要求停止任何活动。该市律师说：“这是关于欺骗的案子。”", "有些法官表示“现在介入还为时过早”。阿利托法官当天没有出席，因为他持有石油公司的股票。判决预计在6月左右做出。", "在最高法院外，人们聚集在一起呼吁“让审判继续”。博尔德市的官员说：“由于气候变化，山火、洪水和酷热越来越严重。难道只有市民自己掏钱吗？”"], "body": "アメリカの最高裁で、月曜日に話し合いがありました。エクソンとサンコーという石油の会社が、「州の法律で会社を裁くのはおかしい」と言いました。会社の弁護士は「一つの市が世界の石油のルールを決めることはできない」と言いました。\n\nボルダーという市は2018年に裁判を始めました。市は「会社は気候についてうそをついた」と言っています。市はお金を求めています。活動を止めてとは言っていません。市の弁護士は「これはうその話です」と言いました。\n\n裁判官の中には「今はまだ早い」と言う人もいました。アリート裁判官はその日いませんでした。かれは石油の株を持っているからです。判決は6月ごろになります。\n\n最高裁の外では、人が集まって「裁判を続けて」と言いました。ボルダーの市の人は「気候のせいで、火事や水や暑さがひどくなっています。お金は市民だけが払うのですか」と言いました。", "body_zh": "美国最高法院周一举行了辩论。埃克森和美孚等石油公司表示：“用州法律来审判公司是不合理的。”公司律师说：“一个城市不能决定全世界的石油规则。”\n\n博尔德市在2018年提起了诉讼。该市称：“公司在气候问题上撒了谎。”该市要求赔偿金，并没有要求停止任何活动。该市律师说：“这是关于欺骗的案子。”\n\n有些法官表示“现在介入还为时过早”。阿利托法官当天没有出席，因为他持有石油公司的股票。判决预计在6月左右做出。\n\n在最高法院外，人们聚集在一起呼吁“让审判继续”。博尔德市的官员说：“由于气候变化，山火、洪水和酷热越来越严重。难道只有市民自己掏钱吗？”", "text": "アメリカの最高裁で、月曜日に話し合いがありました。エクソンとサンコーという石油の会社が、「州の法律で会社を裁くのはおかしい」と言いました。会社の弁護士は「一つの市が世界の石油のルールを決めることはできない」と言いました。 ボルダーという市は2018年に裁判を始めました。市は「会社は気候についてうそをついた」と言っています。市はお金を求めています。活動を止めてとは言っていません。市の弁護士は「これはうその話です」と言いました。 裁判官の中には「今はまだ早い」と言う人もいました。アリート裁判官はその日いませんでした。かれは石油の株を持っているからです。判決は6月ごろになります。 最高裁の外では、人が集まって「裁判を続けて」と言いました。ボルダーの市の人は「気候のせいで、火事や水や暑さがひどくなっています。お金は市民だけが払うのですか」と言いました。", "vocab": [{"word": "石油", "pos": "noun", "zh": "石油", "note": "常见名词，N5 级别。"}, {"word": "会社", "pos": "noun", "zh": "公司", "note": "基础词汇。"}, {"word": "最高裁", "pos": "noun", "zh": "最高法院", "note": "日本常用简称。"}, {"word": "裁判", "pos": "noun", "zh": "审判、官司", "note": "法律相关基础词。"}, {"word": "うそ", "pos": "noun", "zh": "谎言", "note": "常用口语词。"}, {"word": "お金", "pos": "noun", "zh": "钱", "note": "基础词汇。"}, {"word": "気候", "pos": "noun", "zh": "气候", "note": "新闻常见词。"}, {"word": "市民", "pos": "noun", "zh": "市民", "note": "社会类基础词。"}], "title_original": "Big Oil asks Supreme Court to kill climate lawsuits before trial", "summary_original": "Oil companies say federal law should block state climate deception claims.", "body_original": "Avoiding a reckoning\nBig Oil asks Supreme Court to kill climate lawsuits before trial\nOil companies say federal law should block state climate deception claims.\nNicholas Kusnetz, Inside Climate News\n–\nOct 6, 2026 9:29 am\n|\n52\nPeople protest in front of the Supreme Court during arguments in the Suncor v. Boulder case on Monday in Washington, D.C.\nCredit:\nJim Watson/AFP via Getty Images\nPeople protest in front of the Supreme Court during arguments in the Suncor v. Boulder case on Monday in Washington, D.C.\nCredit:\nJim Watson/AFP via Getty Images\nText\nsettings\nStory text\nSize\nSmall\nStandard\nLarge\nWidth\n*\nStandard\nWide\nLinks\nStandard\nOrange\n* Subscribers only\nLearn more\nMinimize to nav\nThe US Supreme Court heard arguments from oil companies on Monday that could help decide the fate of more than two dozen lawsuits accusing the industry of deceiving the public about climate change. The claims seek potentially billions of dollars in damages to help pay the costs imposed by climate-related extreme weather.\nYet the questions before the court did not focus on the merits of these claims but instead on technical legal matters over whether they should proceed at all.\nThe case being argued was brought in 2018 by the city and county of Boulder against ExxonMobil and Suncor Energy in Colorado state court. While the case and dozens like it have survived multiple appeals in state and federal courts, the oil companies are asking the Supreme Court to declare that federal law preempts state and local governments from bringing such claims.\n“This case represents an unprecedented effort to use state law to regulate global conduct,” said Kannon Shanmugam, arguing for Exxon and Suncor. In effect, Shanmugam said, Boulder is attempting to use state law to regulate oil company activity around the world. “If Boulder’s claims are allowed to go forward, some 90,000 municipalities across the country would have the ability to make national and international energy policy by asking juries to impose catastrophic damages on selected fossil fuel producers.”\nBut Boulder and dozens of other plaintiffs have argued their claims do not attempt to impose any limits on oil companies at all—the Boulder case seeks damages, not an injunction on any activity.\n“This case is not about emissions,” said Kevin Russell, who argued the case for Boulder. “It’s about deception.”\nOil companies and conservative groups have been ramping up a campaign to block the lawsuits from proceeding as they inch closer to trial. Some states have enacted laws barring such lawsuits, and Republicans have introduced federal legislation that would shield oil companies from climate liability claims. The Trump administration has also intervened in several state cases, including Boulder’s, to try to block them from moving forward. Many scientists whose work is being cited in the cases\nhave been the subject\nof more than 100 public records requests from conservative groups seeking information about their work and funders, according to Politico.\nThe first question that arose at Monday’s hearing, posed by Justice Clarence Thomas, is whether the court has jurisdiction to hear the case at this stage because no ruling has been issued.\nThe oil companies contend that the proliferating lawsuits are already causing sufficient harm and that the court should decide now whether the cases should proceed. Several justices suggested they thought it might be too early to intervene.\n“I think Boulder’s best shot is on the jurisdictional question, whether this case is too early,” Michael Gerrard, faculty director at the Sabin Center for Climate Change Law at Columbia University, said in an interview.\nThe justices also probed the arguments of whether federal law preempts a state claim about climate change and emissions, either through the Clean Air Act or the Constitution.\nSeveral of the conservative justices seemed inclined to agree that federal law preempts at least some of Boulder’s claims, Gerrard said.\nThe oil companies’ lawyers have argued that the Clean Air Act preempts any claims against oil companies tied to climate pollution. They also argue that the Constitution itself gives the federal government exclusive control over interstate pollution.\nRussell argued that Boulder’s claim does not seek to limit pollution, and that there is nothing in the Clean Air Act or the Constitution that would bar states from taking actions that have a secondary effect of reducing pollution, such as imposing a gas tax or, in this case, seeking damages from oil companies.\nJustice Samuel Alito was not at the hearing after\nrecusing himself last week\n. While Alito gave no explanation for the decision, he had been under pressure to do so because he owns stock in oil companies that are party to other lawsuits that would be affected by the court’s decision.\nAlito’s absence leaves open the possibility of a tie vote, which would allow the case to move forward.\nSupporters of the case held rallies outside the court, calling on the justices to let it proceed.\n“Boulder, like other cities and counties across the country, is confronting realities associated with climate change every day, from wildfires and floods to drought, extreme heat, and growing pressures on our water and infrastructure,” said Boulder’s city manager, Nuria Rivera-Vandermyde, in a statement after the hearing. “The legal question before the court is whether taxpayers must bear these costs alone or must the defendants also pay their fair share for the harms they have caused.”\nMany conservative groups and politicians have filed briefs supporting the oil companies, and the Trump administration presented arguments to the justices on Monday.\nBoulder won supportive briefs from not only climate advocacy groups and Democrats but also many academics and former Environmental Protection Agency officials, including some appointed by Republican presidents.\nMany of the cases brought by states and local governments have been paused pending the outcome of the Supreme Court’s ruling in the Boulder case. The decision, expected by the end of the court’s term in June, could bring those claims to an abrupt halt or set the stage for trials to finally begin.\nNicholas Kusnetz is a reporter for Inside Climate News. Before joining ICN, he worked at the Center for Public Integrity and ProPublica. His work has won numerous awards and citations, including from the Society of Professional Journalists, the Society of Environmental Journalists, the Overseas Press Club, the Society of American Business Editors and Writers, and others. His articles have appeared in more than a dozen publications, including Wired, The Washington Post, Businessweek, The Nation, and The New York Times. Nicholas can be reached on Signal at nkusnetz.15.\nThis story originally appeared on\nInside Climate News\n.\nInside Climate News\nInside Climate News\n52 Comments", "fetched_at": "2026-10-06T16:54:39.032413", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "アメリカ", "s": 0.163, "e": 0.588, "sm": 162, "em": 587, "cs": 0, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "の", "s": 0.6, "e": 0.713, "sm": 600, "em": 712, "cs": 4, "ce": 5, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "最高裁", "s": 0.725, "e": 1.438, "sm": 725, "em": 1437, "cs": 5, "ce": 8, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "で", "s": 1.45, "e": 1.65, "sm": 1450, "em": 1650, "cs": 8, "ce": 9, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "、", "s": 1.65, "e": 1.975, "sm": 1650, "em": 1975, "cs": 9, "ce": 10, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "月曜日", "s": 1.975, "e": 2.6, "sm": 1975, "em": 2600, "cs": 10, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "に", "s": 2.612, "e": 2.737, "sm": 2612, "em": 2737, "cs": 13, "ce": 14, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "話し合い", "s": 2.763, "e": 3.388, "sm": 2762, "em": 3387, "cs": 14, "ce": 18, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "が", "s": 3.4, "e": 3.5, "sm": 3400, "em": 3500, "cs": 18, "ce": 19, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "あり", "s": 3.513, "e": 3.7, "sm": 3512, "em": 3700, "cs": 19, "ce": 21, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "まし", "s": 3.712, "e": 3.938, "sm": 3712, "em": 3937, "cs": 21, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "た", "s": 3.95, "e": 4.175, "sm": 3950, "em": 4175, "cs": 23, "ce": 24, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "。", "s": 4.175, "e": 5.062, "sm": 4175, "em": 5062, "cs":